TI  - A Cosmopolitical Compass to Challenge the Future Development of Social Robotics
AU  - Marechal, Nicolas
T3  - Mindtrek '25
AB  - A key aim of the study of interaction design is the removal of friction between the user and the technology. This stands in contrast to socially and environmentally responsible design approaches, where friction is regarded as a potential asset. Drawing on cinema as a speculative lens, the study examines film props, specifically robots in the film A.I. Artificial Intelligence, to reimagine our relationship with robots. The research challenges dominant paradigms in social robotics by introducing an alternative framework grounded in the concept of a cosmopolitical compass, which foregrounds a pluralism of points of view. Traditionally associated with ecological thought, cosmopolitics foregrounds the entanglement of human and nonhuman actors. This paper extends the concept to robotics, advocating for a reframing of robots not merely as tools but as relational entities shaped through diverse perspectives. Through an analysis of the robots’ characterisation and on-set embodiment, the study highlights how their interactions disrupt human-centred narratives toward new modes of sociability with machines. The unique relationship between the film's two main robots offers a critical entry point for reconsidering the social role of robotics today.
