TI  - Enhancing Food Delivery Efficiency Using Machine Learning and Deep Learning for Predictive Delay Classification
AU  - Fan, Dian
T3  - ICCSMT '25
AB  - This study employs real-world data from a food delivery platform to tackle the challenge of predicting delivery lateness among riders, with the aim of enhancing delivery efficiency and user experience. The research involves meticulous cleaning and preprocessing of raw data, as well as enriching the data dimensions through the incorporation of key features such as geographic block partitioning and predicted delivery speed. To address the issue of class imbalance, a random undersampling strategy is implemented. The study compares the performance of seven machine learning and deep learning models, including Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Stacking, Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM), Deep Neural Network (DNN), along with One-Dimensional Convolutional Neural Network (1D-CNN), in predicting rider tardiness. Model hyperparameters are optimized using Random Search strategy, with the F2 score employed as the primary evaluation metric. The findings reveal that the Stacking ensemble model, following undersampling and hyperparameter optimization, outperforms other individual models in terms of both predictive accuracy and robustness. This study successfully develops an efficient delivery prediction model, offering substantial support for delivery platforms in facilitating intelligent decision-making for riders. It contributes to mitigating social conflicts and resource wastage and provides novel insights for enhancing the accuracy of estimated arrival times on delivery platforms.
