TI  - Decoupled User Preference Enhanced Fake News Detection
AU  - Liu, Hao
T3  - CSAIDE '24
AB  - With the increasing popularity of social media, the dissemination of fake news presents a significant threat to societal stability. Currently, methods for detecting fake news primarily fall into three main categories: content based detection, fact-checking based detection and social context based detection. Content-based approaches focus on textual features, but due to the fact that most fake news is intentionally crafted by malicious users, relying solely on textual features makes it difficult to accurately discern the authenticity of news. Fact-checking methods involve validation of news authenticity by domain experts, yet this approach faces challenges due to the timeliness of news. Social-context-based methods leverage patterns of fake news propagation and background information to identify fake news, but they overlook the influence of news dissemination users. To address the aforementioned issues, we analyzed user information involved in news dissemination and found that users tend to exhibit different patterns in distinguishing between true and false news. Given this premise, we suggest a user tendency-enhanced decoupled graph neural network for detecting fake news. This network separates users engaged in news dissemination into high, medium, and low tendency categories to facilitate the learning of news representations. Backed by experimental results on two widely recognized benchmark datasets, our method showcases state-of-the-art performance. It offers an effective solution to counteract the propagation of fake news.
