T3  - ASENS '24
AB  - In the modern information society, fake news has a significant impact on public opinion and social stability. The diversity and heterogeneity of Internet content require multimodal fusion techniques to detect fake news. Existing models usually link multimodal news features in series, ignoring the correlation and complementarity between modalities. To this end, we propose a multimodal fake news detection method based on multi-view feature fusion and prediction consistency optimization. We model image and text features from unimodal and multimodal perspectives and perform fine-grained fusion from correlation and alignment perspectives. We introduce two classifiers and incorporate the Kullback-Leibler (KL) into the loss function to enhance the consistency of the predictions. Comprehensive tests on two public datasets, Twitter and Pheme, show that our model achieves an accuracy of 90.2% and 90.3% in detecting multimodal fake news, indicating that our model outperforms the baseline model.
