TY  - CONF
TI  - Research on weight leakage input algorithm and artificial intelligence generated text detection
AU  - Fang, Haozhe
AU  - Chen, Weijia
T3  - AISNS '25
AB  - Large language models are capable of generating texts similar to those produced by humans. However, currently, there is still a lack of effective methods to detect the texts generated by large language models. To address this issue, certain modifications are made to the BERT and ROBERTA pre-training models and applied to the identification of texts generated by large language models, enabling them to better adapt to the construction of text identification models for large language models. This can provide ideas for efficiently and high-quality detecting AI-generated texts. In this scenario, the existing models do not pay particular attention to the context relevance when learning texts. Meanwhile, the vocabulary used by AI is getting closer to that of humans, which leads to a further reduction in the inter-class distance between human-created and AI-created texts. In response to this, the LLI Linear Leaky Input algorithm is proposed. This algorithm splits the training texts into multiple central sentences and determines the corresponding environmental sentences for each central sentence. When the training texts enter the input layer of the model, a portion of the weight of the central sentence is leaked into the environmental sentences. This algorithm significantly enhances the model’s ability to identify texts with strong context relevance and optimizes the overall performance of the text identification model for large language models. Without the application of the LLI algorithm, the model performance is far lower than that of the best model for identifying texts generated by large language models, chatgpt-detector-roberta, tested by Kavita Kumari et al. However, after applying the LLI algorithm with the selected LLI parameters (1, 1), the model performance is significantly improved. Eventually, under the same circumstances, the performance of the model optimized by the LLI algorithm is far superior to that of chatgpt-detector-roberta, with the Fscore relatively increased by 55.07%.
