TY  - CONF
TI  - Fake News Detection Utilizing Social Context Information with Graph Convolutional Networks and Attention Mechanisms
AU  - Yan, Facheng
AU  - Zhang, Mingshu
AU  - Wei, Bin
AU  - Jiang, Wen
AU  - Ren, Kelan
T3  - EITCE '23
AB  - Fake news has emerged as a significant societal issue, eroding public trust in the government and news media, while also inciting public panic and triggering social chaos. However, relying solely on the news itself for fake news detection is inadequate due to its deceptive nature. To complement and enhance fake news detection, it is necessary to incorporate social context information related to the news. This paper proposes a fake news detection framework based on graph convolutional networks and attention mechanisms. By representing the news and its social context information (such as participating users and comments) as a heterogeneous graph, we employ meta-paths to capture the relationships between news-comments and news-users respectively. Graph convolutional networks are utilized to effectively extract meaningful social context information, while attention mechanisms are employed to learn the importance of various types of social context information, thereby acquiring informative representations of news node features. Experiments conducted on publicly available datasets validate the effectiveness of the proposed framework.
