TI  - Blocking Methods of Network Rumor Spreading Paths Based on Deep Learning
AU  - Xu, Le
T3  - BDNNDL '25
AB  - This paper discusses the blocking method of network rumor spreading path based on deep learning, aiming at solving the problems of response delay and inefficiency of traditional rumor governance methods. The research focuses on the automatic identification and intervention of four types of rumors, namely, high-risk emergency, social contradiction, property fraud and general misleading. By integrating multi-source heterogeneous data, including rumor text content, transmission dynamic time series data and publisher behavior portraits, a classification and blocking framework that integrates convolutional neural network and long-term and short-term memory network is constructed. The model uses CNN to extract the spatial features of text semantics, LSTM to capture the temporal dynamics of propagation paths, and cross-modal attention mechanism to deeply integrate multi-dimensional information to achieve end-to-end rumor classification. The empirical analysis shows that the performance of the proposed model is significantly better than mainstream baseline algorithm in the identification of four types of rumors, and its key advantage lies in the ability to capture key features such as the combination of sensitive words and the mutation of propagation rate, with the recall rate of high-risk and urgent rumors reaching 0.932 and the AUC value of fraudulent property rumors rising to 0.885. It provides efficient and accurate intelligent decision support for cyberspace governance, and promotes the construction of a clear network environment.
