TI  - Predicting Low-carbon Practice: Integrating SEM and Machine Learning Validation of Extended Norm Activation Model
AU  - Fu, Da
AU  - Shaharudin, Muhammad Shabir
AU  - Huang, Yuhui
AU  - Chen, Yujie
AU  - Wei, Xingxiong
AU  - Wei, Bing
T2  - Proceedings of the 2025 2nd International Conference on Big Data and Digital Management
AB  - This study investigates the formation mechanism of residents' low-carbon practice by extending the Norm Activation Model (NAM) with social norms and external factors, validated through machine learning approaches. Based on survey data from 832 residents in Yunnan Province, China, we employed structural equation modeling to examine relationships among low-carbon awareness, knowledge, norms, and practice. Results reveal that external factors exert the strongest influence on low-carbon practice (β=0.401, r=0.452), followed by personal norms (β=0.217) and social norms (β=0.117), with knowledge showing a stronger impact than awareness on personal norms. Multi-group analysis demonstrates significant demographic variations, particularly stronger norm effects among women and younger residents. The Random Forest model validates the theoretical framework with high predictive accuracy (R²=0.773), confirming that selected variables explain 77.3% of variance in low-carbon practice. This integration provides robust evidence for understanding the cognitive-behavioral gap and offers reliable support for developing targeted sustainable lifestyle promotion strategies.
