TI  - Deep Learning-Based Attribution Framework for Real-Time Budget Optimization in Cross-Channel Pharmaceutical Advertising: A Comparative Study of Traditional and Digital Channels
AU  - Zhang, Jin
T3  - SECA '25
AB  - This article makes a case for real-time budget adjustments, which should be based on your ad's performance. Regarding customer journeys and their relation with different channels, regular marketing attribution models do not stand a chance, not in a strict regulatory context. In agreement with the current state of the art: explainable machine learning and multilingual sentiment analysis, the solution addresses the incoherent data (many such actions happen infrequently), the delayed conversions, and the global market diversity. In this post, we use multi-head attention bidirectional LSTM networks to quantify the credit that each marketing touchpoint receives, but also across digital and to the online touchpoints within traditional channels. By combining Federated Learning and SHAP interpretability, the framework provides compliance with privacy and transparency in model decision-making. Experiments demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves significant gains in attribution accuracy and resource allocation efficiency over baseline models. Furthermore, multilingual lessons in sentiment signals, coupled with conversion histories of users, provide insight into both when and to what extent one should be adaptively managing budget in response to market variation. In this post, we present a scalable, open, and transparent solution for pharmaceutical advertising COE managers to maximize the ROI of their promotions across this incredibly diverse group of markets.
