TI  - Mitigating Class Imbalance in Fact-Checking Datasets Through LLM-Based Synthetic Data Generation
AU  - Moe, Lwin
AU  - Nguyen, Uyen Trang
AU  - Luu, Boi Trinh
T3  - MAD' 25
AB  - The proliferation of disinformation in digital media has prompted significant advances in automated systems for verifying content veracity. Evidence-based fact-checking leverages natural language processing (NLP) and deep learning to assess the veracity of textual claims by comparing them against relevant evidence. However, fact-checking datasets frequently exhibit class imbalance, which can cause machine learning models to favor the majority class and perform poorly on the minority class. In this paper, we address the class imbalance issue in fact-checking tasks by using large language models (LLMs), such as LLaMA 3 and Mistral-7B, to generate additional samples for the minority class. We evaluate our approach by training fact-checking models on both the original dataset and several augmented datasets, then comparing their performance. Experimental results demonstrate that LLM-generated data significantly enhances the precision, recall, and F1 score for the minority class, without compromising overall accuracy. This approach offers a promising solution to the class imbalance problem in training fact-checking models. Our work contributes to enhancing the robustness of automated disinformation detection systems and improving the effectiveness of automated fact-checking in real-world applications.
