AB  - There has been a recent growth in interest surrounding the identification of misinformation in online news, specifically in the case of political news. This task was previously human-based, one which has various amounts of efficacy and time commitment for teams of fact-checkers taking up to a week to check the veracity of a single statement. Given recent research and development in artificial intelligence, this is no longer the case. This research has led to a plethora of ideas and techniques to create an ideal fact-checking in an automatic manner, which autonomously discerns the veracity of a given statement quickly. A tool like this is a massive boon to teams of fact-checkers, helping them expedite the process and allowing any individual to check claims made online. This paper will explore various supervised machine learning approaches for this intended purpose and how they interact with numerical features extracted from a dataset of journalistic writing focusing on the efficacy and efficiency thereof.
