TI  - Climate Change Myths Detection Using Dynamically Weighted Ensemble Based Stance Classifier
AU  - Mazid, Md Abdullah Al
AU  - Zarnaz, Zaima
T3  - ICCA '22
AB  - Fake news in social media is currently one of the major obstacles that stand in the way of mass-scale climate action. To identify problematic posts that agree with misconceptions regarding climate change or global warming, we developed a dynamically weighted ensemble-based stance classifier capable of classifying the stance a piece of text holds towards a specified target. Our stance classifier has proven to be extremely successful and has exceeded the accuracies of all previous stance classifiers by a large margin in classifying ’agree’ and ’disagree’ stances. The characteristic feature of the ensemble is that weights are dynamically assigned to the constituent models based on their chosen stance for the post to be categorized and the accuracy with which the base model can classify that particular stance. The model is accompanied by a large dataset comprised of the most popular myths regarding climate change as targets along with a significant number of associated posts containing the target that can have one of four possible stances towards its associated myth including: ’agree’, ’disagree’, ’discuss’, and ’unrelated’. The proposed model is capable of understanding even difficult aspects of human conversation such as humor and sarcasm. It has achieved an overall accuracy of 82.03%.
