AU  - Doble, Francis
AU  - Gerardino, Gabriel Angelo
AU  - Santos, Jaira
AU  - Tiam-Lee, Thomas James
T3  - NLPIR '24
AB  - Advances in natural language processing have significantly enhanced the analysis of large-scale text data, offering new insights for sociological research. This study leverages BERT-based models to analyze audience comments in Pinoybaiting videos, a concept in which foreign content creators produce exaggerated positive reactions to aspects of Filipino culture such as food, music, and tourist destinations, allegedly with exploitative intentions. Previous scholarly attempts to understand Pinoybaiting are limited to small samples and manual qualitative techniques. Our research is the first wide-scale study on this social phenomenon, comprising 8,288,591 comments from 42,485 YouTube videos across a 7-year time period. Two domain-specific BERT-based models were trained to classify these comments according to sentiment and intention, achieving an overall accuracy of 73.71% and 80.10%, respectively. We found that despite the negative allegations against such videos, majority of the comments are positive in nature, although there is a slight downward trend in positive types of comments through time. Furthermore, we found that some types of videos tend to be associated with certain types of comments.
