AU  - Zhang, Xiyan
T3  - BDE '25
AB  - Climate change is a globally debated topic that often polarizes public opinion. This study presents a stance and sentiment classification framework for climate change discussions on Twitter, leveraging fine-tuned BERT models. By preprocessing over 5,000 climate-related tweets, the proposed system achieves promising results in classifying both sentiment (positive, negative, neutral) and stance (support, deny, neutral). The experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of deep learning in extracting nuanced emotional and attitudinal signals from social media texts. This framework contributes to scalable social opinion mining on large unstructured datasets, supporting future research on environmental discourse analysis. While the proposed framework achieves an improvement of approximately 9–40% over traditional baselines, this performance should be contextualized against recent transformer-based climate discourse benchmarks, where larger pre-trained models or domain-specific variants often report stronger gains. Our contribution lies in demonstrating that even a compact model like BERT-base-uncased, when paired with domain-adapted preprocessing, can achieve competitive performance on noisy climate-related text.
