TI  - DERM: Dynamic Evidence Reliability Modeling for Multimodal Rumor Detection
AU  - Pan, Zhuangzhuang
AU  - Xia, Yan
AU  - Balakrishnan, Vimala
AU  - Zainal Abidin, Muhammad Zaiamri
AU  - Abdul Rahman, Siti Soraya
T3  - MMAsia '25
AB  - Multimodal rumor detection with external evidence faces the challenge of dynamically assessing evidence of varying quality and relevance. We propose Dynamic Evidence Reliability Modeling (DERM), a framework that unifies evidence quality assessment and semantic relevance evaluation through information-theoretic principles. DERM introduces three synergistic components: (1) An information density gating mechanism that distinguishes high-quality evidence via cross-modal consistency; (2) A Semantic Alignment Module that adjusts evidence weights by jointly considering information density and claim-evidence similarity; (3) A multi-objective optimization with alignment regularization that promotes sparse attention while preserving semantic grounding. Experiments on Weibo-MR2 and Twitter-MR2 demonstrate DERM’s state-of-the-art performance. Our method achieves 91.4% and 87.8% accuracy, outperforming the strongest baseline by 0.9 and 0.5 points. Notably, DERM boosts rumor class F1 scores by 0.4 points on both datasets, reaching 88.2% and 75.5%. Visualizations further confirm our design: high-quality evidence receives nearly three times more attention than noisy counterparts, enhancing interpretability.
