TI  - Combating Dark-side of Computing - Exploring Perceptions of AI-augmented Mis/disinformation among Higher Education Students
AU  - Martin, Florence
AU  - DeLarm, Jennifer
AU  - Zhuang, Min
AU  - Cheng, Yang
AU  - Rand, William
AU  - Lee, Jaekuk
T3  - ICER '25
AB  - Computing Education (CE) is not only about programming or computer science, it also includes the broader socio-technical phenomena surrounding computing such as privacy, security, and information ethics [5, 7]. Moreover, it should go beyond emphasizing the capabilities and efficiencies of computing to critically engage with its risks, particularly in the context of evolving AI technologies that amplify both the benefits and potential harms of computing [3, 4]. One pressing concern is the rise of deepfake technologies, which challenge our ability to distinguish between authentic and manipulated content, resulting in the spread of mis- and disinformation and eroding public trust [1, 2, 6]. As such, raising awareness of these risks, and developing the skills necessary to detect and combat AI-generated disinformation must become essential components of CE. The primary goal of this Computing Education Research (CER) is to examine higher education students’ experiences, perceptions, and behaviors when confronted with deep fake content. The study aims to derive a conceptual framework representing students’ current knowledge and attitudes toward this issue and to inform the design of future CE interventions on AI-augmented mis and disinformation.We conducted five focus group interviews with both graduate and undergraduate students. A total of 31 higher education students participated, with group sizes ranging from 4 to 8. A semi-structured interview protocol was developed to guide the discussions and elicit rich responses. Participants were prompted to reflect on their experiences and share their perceptions related (1) the generation and detection of misinformation and disinformation, (2) associated risks and consequences, (3) the role of AI-based technologies, and (4) the academic environment, training, and future support needed to address AI-generated mis- and disinformation.Based on the preliminary inductive qualitative coding [8], several themes under each topic were identified and detailed in Table. 1. The findings aim to inform strategies for addressing the growing societal challenge of mis- and disinformation especially in the context of deepfakes, and contribute to the advancement of CE, particularly in areas of information and cybersecurity education.
