TY  - CONF
TI  - TSTR for Financial Fraud: Learning to Detect Manipulation Without Real Data
AU  - Mahrous, Ahmed
AU  - Di Pietro, Roberto
T3  - ICAIF '25
AB  - One of the main challenges in financial data analysis using supervised machine learning techniques is the scarcity of labeled real-world data. For instance, this issue is quite compelling in the context of detecting market manipulation, such as pump-and-dump schemes. In this paper, we address the cited issue by introducing the train-on-synthetic, test-on-real (TSTR) paradigm. We generate synthetic pump-and-dump time series using Gaussian Sampling, Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), and Diffusion Models, and train a Rocket classifier exclusively on this data. Our results show that training on synthetic data alone achieves a detection accuracy of 79.75%, and adding just 20% real data raises it to 88.1%—close to the performance achieved by training on real data alone. Notably, Gaussian sampling outperforms all deep-learning generators, challenging the assumption that model complexity correlates with utility. Furthermore, we demonstrate that TSTR exposes a mismatch between conventional similarity metrics and the practical utility of synthetic data. The proposed framework is novel and, as shown by our extensive experimental campaign, viable. Finally, it is applicable to a broad class of data-scarce financial anomaly detection problems.
