TI  - Deepfake Laws Protecting Childhood Safety and Future: An NLP Analysis
AU  - Roundtree, Aimee Kendall
T2  - Proceedings of the 24th Interaction Design and Children
AB  - This paper analyzes emerging U.S. legislation aimed at protecting minors from harm caused by deepfake technologies. Drawing on state and federal bills introduced between 2024 and 2025, the study uses natural language processing (NLP) and linguistic analysis to examine excerpts referencing deepfakes and youth. The findings reveal that some bills emphasize criminal penalties, while others address civil remedies, age-based definitions, and technological safeguards. Sentiment and thematic analysis show how lawmakers frame cognitive, emotional, and moral dimensions of deepfake-related harms. The study builds on prior literature that calls for more targeted, enforceable, and ethically grounded legal responses to synthetic media. The findings also highlight inconsistencies and gaps in coverage and enforcement. The paper concludes with recommendations for policy standardization, interdisciplinary collaboration, and expanded protections for children in digital environments shaped by synthetic content.
