TI  - A Study on the Influencing Factors of AIGC-Generated News Content Adoption Based on Audience Perception
AU  - Cao, Huining
T3  - MLSC '25
AB  - With the increasing integration of artificial intelligence in the news industry, this study explores the influence of audience perception on the adoption intention of AI-generated content (AIGC). Through data analysis of 172 valid questionnaires, the study validated three hypotheses (H1–H3) based on value perception, credibility perception, and social influence perception. The results indicate that value perception (P = 0.001) and social influence perception (P = 0.002) have a significant positive impact on adoption intent, while credibility perception (P = 0.090) does not significantly influence adoption intent. This suggests that although audiences have concerns about the credibility of AIGC news, these concerns do not significantly hinder their adoption intentions. Further analysis revealed that AIGC lagged behind traditional news in terms of information completeness (P = 0.001), content depth (P &lt; 0.001), and credibility (P &lt; 0.001), but these perceived differences did not significantly affect adoption intention (P = 0.150). The study results provide empirical support for optimising AIGC content, suggesting that strengthening information value and social communication strategies can enhance audience trust and acceptance.
